reformulations.equivalence_argument:
T ↦ T+1 is a bijection from subsets of ℕ₀ to subsets of the positive integers, with (T+1)(x) (counted on [1,x]) = T(x−1) (counted on [0,x]).

The shift preserves:
- additive complementarity, since (A+1)+(B+1) = A+B+2;
- infinitude;
- limsup A(x)B(x)/x, since the ratio picks up a factor (x−1)/x → 1;
- the conclusion "→ +∞", since the new A(x)B(x) − x equals the old value at x−1 minus 1.

Sequences reduce to sets. Replacing a sequence by its set of values keeps the sumset and infinitude (each value occurs finitely often). It does not increase A(x)B(x), so limsup < 3/2 and the failure of A(x)B(x) − x → ∞ both survive. This is the same reduction Chen–Fang use in [ChFa15, §2]. Hence a counterexample in either setting gives one in the other.
